-- Q5002: Entity Formation and Restructuring [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Circumventing the continuity of interest requirement through a tax-free distribution of subsidiary stock","Using a corporate division principally as a device to distribute earnings and profits as capital gains instead of dividends","Transferring assets between related corporations to avoid gain recognition on appreciated property","Acquiring a loss corporation solely to utilize its net operating loss carryforwards"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 355(a)(1)(B), the device test prevents taxpayers from using spin-offs, split-offs, or split-ups principally as a device for distributing corporate earnings and profits to shareholders at capital gains rates rather than as ordinary dividends. The IRS examines factors such as the presence of a subsequent sale and the nature of the businesses. (Sec. 355(a)(1)(B).)
Wrong (A): Continuity of interest is a separate judicial doctrine applicable to reorganizations, not specifically the target of the device test.
Wrong (C): Asset transfers between related corporations are addressed by other code provisions, not the Section 355 device test.
Wrong (D): Acquisition of loss corporations to use NOLs is addressed by Section 269 and Section 382, not the device test.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 5002;

-- Q5003: Entity Formation and Restructuring [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A Type B stock-for-stock reorganization, preserving the target''s historical asset basis","A Type A statutory merger structured without boot to maintain tax-free treatment","A direct taxable asset purchase from the target corporation at fair market value","A taxable stock purchase with a Section 338(h)(10) election treating the deal as a deemed asset sale"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 338(h)(10), a buyer who acquires a target''s stock can elect to treat the purchase as a deemed asset sale, obtaining a stepped-up basis in the target''s assets. The stock sale is disregarded and only the deemed asset sale is recognized on the old target''s final return, avoiding the double tax that occurs in a direct asset purchase. (Sec. 338(h)(10).)
Wrong (A): A Type B reorganization is tax-free, so the buyer inherits the target''s carryover basis and does not obtain a step-up.
Wrong (B): A Type A merger without boot is also tax-free, preserving carryover basis in the target''s assets.
Wrong (C): A direct taxable asset purchase triggers two levels of tax — corporate-level gain plus shareholder-level gain on liquidation — making it costlier than a 338(h)(10) election.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 5003;

-- Q5953: Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Any nonrecourse debt secured by personal property used in a trade or business","Nonrecourse debt guaranteed by a federal agency regardless of the collateral type","Recourse debt obtained from a related party lender for any business activity","Nonrecourse financing from a qualified lender secured by real property used in the activity"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under Section 465(b)(6), qualified nonrecourse financing is nonrecourse debt from a qualified lender (such as a bank or government agency) that is secured by real property used in the activity of holding real property. This exception allows real estate investors to include certain nonrecourse debt in their at-risk amounts. (Sec. 465(b)(6).)
Wrong (A): The qualified nonrecourse financing exception applies only to real property, not personal property.
Wrong (B): While government-backed debt may qualify, the collateral must be real property used in the activity — the collateral requirement cannot be disregarded.
Wrong (C): Recourse debt from a related party is a separate concept; qualified nonrecourse financing must be nonrecourse and from an unrelated qualified lender.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 5953;

